### Hard C++ Problem: Implementing a Least Recently Used (LRU) Cache
#### Problem Statement
Implement a Least Recently Used (LRU) cache using C++. The cache should have a fixed capacity and should support two operations: `get` and `put`.

*   `get(key)`: Returns the value of the key if it exists in the cache; otherwise, returns -1.
*   `put(key, value)`: Inserts or updates the value of the key in the cache. If the key already exists, update its value. If the key does not exist and the cache has reached its capacity, remove the least recently used key before inserting the new key.

#### Example
```cpp
LRUCache cache = new LRUCache(2); // Capacity of 2

cache.put(1, 1);
cache.put(2, 2);
System.out.println(cache.get(1)); // Returns 1
cache.put(3, 3); // Evicts key 2
System.out.println(cache.get(2)); // Returns -1 (not found)
cache.put(4, 4); // Evicts key 1
System.out.println(cache.get(1)); // Returns -1 (not found)
System.out.println(cache.get(3)); // Returns 3
System.out.println(cache.get(4)); // Returns 4
```

#### Solution
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <unordered_map>

class LRUCache {
private:
    int capacity;
    std::list<std::pair<int, int>> cache;
    std::unordered_map<int, std::list<std::pair<int, int>>::iterator> map;

public:
    LRUCache(int capacity) : capacity(capacity) {}

    int get(int key) {
        if (map.find(key) != map.end()) {
            auto it = map[key];
            int value = it->second;
            cache.erase(it);
            cache.push_back({key, value});
            map[key] = --cache.end();
            return value;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    void put(int key, int value) {
        if (map.find(key) != map.end()) {
            cache.erase(map[key]);
        } else if (cache.size() >= capacity) {
            map.erase(cache.front().first);
            cache.pop_front();
        }
        cache.push_back({key, value});
        map[key] = --cache.end();
    }
};

int main() {
    LRUCache cache(2);
    cache.put(1, 1);
    cache.put(2, 2);
    std::cout << cache.get(1) << std::endl; // Returns 1
    cache.put(3, 3); // Evicts key 2
    std::cout << cache.get(2) << std::endl; // Returns -1 (not found)
    cache.put(4, 4); // Evicts key 1
    std::cout << cache.get(1) << std::endl; // Returns -1 (not found)
    std::cout << cache.get(3) << std::endl; // Returns 3
    std::cout << cache.get(4) << std::endl; // Returns 4
    return 0;
}
```

#### Explanation

*   We use an unordered map (`std::unordered_map`) to store the keys and their corresponding iterators in the list. This allows us to quickly find and update the position of a key in the list.
*   We use a doubly-linked list (`std::list`) to store the key-value pairs. This allows us to efficiently insert and remove elements from the list.
*   In the `get` function, we check if the key exists in the map. If it does, we update its position in the list by erasing the old iterator and inserting a new one at the end of the list.
*   In the `put` function, we check if the key already exists in the map. If it does, we update its value and position in the list. If it does not exist and the cache has reached its capacity, we remove the least recently used key (the front element of the list) before inserting the new key.

### Time Complexity

*   `get`: O(1) on average, O(n) in the worst case (when the key is not found and the cache is full)
*   `put`: O(1) on average, O(n) in the worst case (when the cache is full and the key is not found)

### Space Complexity

*   O(n) for storing the key-value pairs in the list and the map

